{"title":"2007 BMO Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_BMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(<i>Serbia</i>)\nFind all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that there exists a permutation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/3/fa35d9fc104207e09a712110ac81612c5b279a6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sigma$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> on the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/f/dbff5e702f9ce665315ddfe211a79dc98ab0b278.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{ 1, \\ldots, n \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> for which\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/1/a511e123fa8259afad8597ac800d0b2649b2abd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{\\sigma(1) + \\sqrt{\\sigma(2) + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{\\sigma(n)}}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"266\" height=\"66\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>is a rational number.\n</p><p><i><b>Note</b>: A permutation of the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/9/099d5203905994879e5da1c8e7eba45decf09e79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" /> is a one-to-one function of this set to itself.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_BMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The only solutions are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/9/1d96ecc15129c5631ba4a2c6578e91e3d13e3baf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/3/5635737307f5f0651cced8ee2e6558a426fd27b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>First, we will prove that for positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/f/67f9dc5bf16a2a3ab21a3de64406b0637de9a779.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, \\ldots, a_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"78\" height=\"11\" />, the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/6/5d6cac3cff5dad3b90254bad0e1115a318a8c001.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{a_m + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"156\" height=\"43\" /> is rational if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/c/98cb04e8a9eb1a18eb73e395a4689e196b6856c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{a_i + \\sqrt{ + \\cdots  + \\sqrt{a_1}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"166\" height=\"43\" /> is an integer, for all integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/a/38a0713e8fbcb58f5f02d1bcdfe8057f5c53beb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\le i \\le m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"79\" height=\"15\" />.  This follows from induction on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />.  The case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b5800f5ad040d308609190ef18a9e0ca08a9b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> is trivial; now, suppose it holds for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/8/c884e65ff234251cef8e6979401dd69dd900a842.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, \\ldots, a_{m-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"11\" />.  Then if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/8/7282511a6f45261758e36add1bd65a2179b050f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{a_m + \\sqrt{a_{m-1} + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"235\" height=\"53\" /> is an rational, than its square, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/7/a77a4c0b08868bb7050af98d54b8bff0427396c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_m + \\sqrt{a_{m-1} + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"219\" height=\"45\" /> must also be rational, which implies that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/4/f1462e623a0a7de3250fe8c5cea260da7d0ca3ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{a_{m-1} + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"173\" height=\"43\" /> is rational.  But by the inductive hypothesis, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/4/f1462e623a0a7de3250fe8c5cea260da7d0ca3ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{a_{m-1} + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"173\" height=\"43\" /> must be an integer, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/7/a77a4c0b08868bb7050af98d54b8bff0427396c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_m + \\sqrt{a_{m-1} + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"219\" height=\"45\" /> must also be an integer, which means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/8/7282511a6f45261758e36add1bd65a2179b050f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{a_m + \\sqrt{a_{m-1} + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"235\" height=\"53\" /> is also an integer, since it is rational.  The result follows.\n</p><p>Next, we prove that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/8/02841e1c208b6f54089adb11cc9cce42d278bd2f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, \\ldots, a_m, k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"97\" height=\"16\" /> are positive integers such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/6/2a6628ad40ba88d22d04658b45e402f4df370abc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, \\ldots, a_m \\le k^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"120\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/6/5d6cac3cff5dad3b90254bad0e1115a318a8c001.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{a_m + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"156\" height=\"43\" /> is rational, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/6/d669cf8d5e99b8200aa8fbfd468e3d9cc9bd4c33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{a_m + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}} \\le k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"189\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p><p>This also comes by induction.  The case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b5800f5ad040d308609190ef18a9e0ca08a9b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> is trivial.  If our result holds for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/8/c884e65ff234251cef8e6979401dd69dd900a842.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, \\ldots, a_{m-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"11\" />, then\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/d/66dd744284fc02e7021cf4e0ad99f29544dd02c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{a_m + \\sqrt{a_{m-1} + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}}} \\le \\sqrt{k^2 + k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"324\" height=\"53\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/7/157f67e02fc592ff3d6cd663b992a714a47ea91d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_m + \\sqrt{a_{m-1} + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{a_1}}} \\le k^2 + k &lt; (k+1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"376\" height=\"43\" /> must be a perfect square, it can be at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/5/0e5c83cc0c639fc2ed843bbe2d3d209fb478b104.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" />, and the result follows.\n</p><p>Now we prove that no <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/e/a1e903a5a7c9e6f17b32bf920da41cdb640afbd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" /> is a solution.\n</p><p>Suppose that there is some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/e/a1e903a5a7c9e6f17b32bf920da41cdb640afbd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" /> that is a solution.  Than there exists some number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/6/056f528dc0800bed410ba4bc698d4b6560b7f3c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m^2+1 \\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"89\" height=\"17\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/4/aa42cfae2a516a7693bfbd48425474fbc75d5be0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\le (m+1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"106\" height=\"21\" />.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/e/a1e903a5a7c9e6f17b32bf920da41cdb640afbd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/7/057321cffed3945e905ff058f29f7393602bcc2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\ge 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" />.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/b/63b2dff8c47bd58112e66110992370a81716bded.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m^2+1 =\\sigma(i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"19\" />, then we know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/8/2487c47c8f61645b4b81fe8be841023a87494262.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i \\neq n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"17\" /> since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/d/1fd88d63cb17674bf7deb772586f14aed2318005.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m^2 + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"16\" /> is not a square; and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/b/3cbedd5502d9c0924e5ce1de12ec4940a46169ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sigma(i) + \\sqrt{\\sigma(i+1) + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{\\sigma(n)}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"272\" height=\"53\" /> must be a perfect square, so we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/0/0b0c4594f46379c68895d1eade2fa67f50b2fee4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{ \\sigma(i+1) + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{ \\sigma(n)}}} \\ge 2m &gt; m+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"337\" height=\"53\" />.  But this is a contradiction.\n</p><p>We now have the cases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/2/ea256dfe34c6eedd4d1e350fb397cd824dddc15d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1,2,3,4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"16\" /> to consider.  The case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/9/1d96ecc15129c5631ba4a2c6578e91e3d13e3baf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> is trivial.  For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/b/6cb7ae00764b56ee2adf59e78a1ffde9685b80db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> it is easy to verify that neither <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/d/28d53d73ee0ba55d322b27f850e83387e63681d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"74\" height=\"32\" /> nor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/7/9b7a2d18f59b387e8e4f9f26c4558655ca2d3859.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{1 + \\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"75\" height=\"35\" /> is rational.  For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/3/5635737307f5f0651cced8ee2e6558a426fd27b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, we have the solution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/5/3058536571311881ab44fcf0e093de4edb4dd407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{ 2 + \\sqrt{ 3 + \\sqrt{1}}} = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -9px\" width=\"155\" height=\"43\" />.  We now consider. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/c/39cf3e35a4981583288d2a7c4b34989916fb7360.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" />.  First, suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/6/07673b05d0bba8090a36babef9cf459d5fdb0333.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 \\neq \\sigma(4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />; say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/c/f4cc29ef1bfea029c5e94b64468781dc6d874f90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sigma(i) = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" />.  We have already shown that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4cf4bbf5456024a7e74b0dc9d6f34113c349b08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x= \\sqrt{ \\sigma(i+1) + \\sqrt{ \\cdots + \\sqrt{\\sigma(4)}}} \\le 2 &lt; 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"319\" height=\"56\" />; this means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/1/b615dd42158acadf00604da355bafeed07aa6661.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 &lt; \\sigma(i)+x &lt;9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"130\" height=\"18\" />, a contradiction, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6c55896907f771c33b3d3a1038f47844ab7473f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sigma(i) + x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" /> must be a perfect square.  Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/81878d274d3a7ca4e2486c9a046408e698c58e7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sigma(4) =4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" />.  But here we have either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e25b1a44975bbc9bba4cd83d098c74726e2d7b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{1 + \\sqrt{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"74\" height=\"32\" /> is irrational, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/c/e9c5f8fb8060b2cad1affdee38e9e3073d2ceba6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3 + \\sqrt{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"74\" height=\"32\" /> is irrational, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/0/d90f35da275c27a7b4ddf26023a679f51afd2d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{1 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{4}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -9px\" width=\"123\" height=\"43\" /> is irrational, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/0/06094bcf283a5e79c6b4f54d60c346d24f5a2919.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{4}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -9px\" width=\"123\" height=\"43\" /> is irrational.  Thus there is no solution for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/c/39cf3e35a4981583288d2a7c4b34989916fb7360.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> and the only solutions are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/9/8499b7e0bb968592aa45e44c9ac2156e87ce5e25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1, 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p><i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2007_BMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_BMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_BMO_Problems\" title=\"2007 BMO Problems\">2007 BMO Problems</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?p=827183#827183\">Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017213646\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.026 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.039 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 315/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 344/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 11591/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.513      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n100.00%    5.513      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4912:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017213646 and revision id 227289. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}